 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "SNAB.3 (learning outcome) · Valuing works of art and artists — makes predictions about how works are made (“How do you paint on water?”); recognises traditional works (ebru, paper marbling).",
   "SNAB.2 (learning outcome) · Examining a work of art — SNAB.2.g expresses feelings and thoughts about the work and explains the reasons: the child describes what they see in Elif's tulip ebru and how it makes them feel.",
   "SNAB.4 (learning outcome; wording unverified) · Carrying out an art activity — decides on the type of art activity and chooses the materials needed: Elif makes her own ebru with a brush, paints, a water tray and paper; the child follows the same steps in class.",
   "skill: SNAB4 Artistic Practice · OB5 Cultural Literacy — getting to know and experiencing cultural heritage such as ebru.",
   "SDB1.2 Self-Regulation · Value: D12 Patience (also D7 Aesthetics) — Elif notices her anger, calms down with a deep breath, finds her own way of slowing down with “snail hands” and learns to wait."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Point to the tray on the cover: ask “What's in the tray? Can you paint on water?”; accept the guesses.",
   "Note for the teacher: Ebru is a traditional art of ours, added to UNESCO's list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2014; if possible, show photos of an ebru tray, a horsehair brush and a rose-twig comb.",
   "If possible, show a real ebru or a photo of one: “How do you think these patterns were made?”",
   "Open a short chat: “Has a job ever gone wrong because you rushed?”"
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, bring the rain to life by tapping your fingertips on the table, “pitter patter”; find the snail on the window together and walk your finger slowly, “slowly, slowly”.",
   "On page 2, say “Splat! Splat! Splat!” fast, and the “Drip!” sounds on page 4 slowly; let the child feel the difference. Look at Elif's face: “Why did her cheeks go hot? What happens in your body when you get cross?”",
   "On page 3, breathe in through the nose and out through the mouth twice, together with Elif. Then ask what Elif is looking at: the snail on the window! Praise both ideas in the prediction question.",
   "On page 4, wait one breath for each colour while counting the drops: “Snail hands!”",
   "On page 5, touch the brush first, then Grandma's paper, and last the tray; count slowly together, “One… two… three…”, then turn the page."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Why did Elif think it hadn't worked the first time? What changed the second time?",
   "What do “snail hands” mean? What other jobs could you use your snail hands for?",
   "What is in Elif's ebru? Which colour did you like most, and why?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Colour Dance with Snail Hands",
   "sure": "25 min",
   "materyal": "Wide, shallow trays; a child-friendly ebru kit (thickened water and ebru paints) or shaving foam and watered-down watercolours; droppers or brushes; toothpicks or a comb; A5 paper; aprons",
   "adim": [
    "The teacher prepares the trays; talk with the children about the rule “Water doesn't like hurry” and that paints never go in the mouth.",
    "Each child drips three to five drops with “snail hands”; with each drop they say “Drip!” and wait one breath.",
    "They slowly pull the colours with a toothpick or comb to make waves or flowers.",
    "They lay the paper on the water, count “One… two… three…” all together, hold the paper by its edge and lift it slowly.",
    "Set up a little exhibition with the dried ebrus; each child says what they see in their work and how it makes them feel."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Which art did Grandma show Elif?",
   "How did Elif calm down when she got cross?",
   "Is there a job you do slowly and patiently too?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Colour Dance in the Kitchen",
   "d": "With an adult, put a little water or milk in a flat plate and add three or four drops of food colouring. Let the child gently touch the middle with a cotton bud dipped in washing-up liquid and watch the colours dance. Say “Drip!” together with each drop and wait one breath; then give the pattern you see a name."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Colours on the Water”. Elif makes ebru with her grandma for the first time; when she rushes, the paints mix and she gets very cross. She takes a deep breath, finds her own way of slowing down with “snail hands” and patiently makes an ebru with tulips. At home, when your child gets impatient with a task, you can smile and ask “Where are your snail hands?” and take a breath together. You can also share stories known in your family about ebru or other traditional arts."
 },
